\par\par \b \uc2 Chip Makers Cash In At Everyone's Expense -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 948 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2026\par 08:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2026, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 We are witnessing an extraordinary transfer of cash from the providers of AI -- and, perhaps one day, AI users -- to memory-chip makers\par \par \uc2 By James Mackintosh\par \par \uc2 The explosive growth in \uc2 Micron Technology\uc2 's profit in the latest quarter is extraordinarily good news for its shareholders, but it comes at the expense of the \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial-intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  companies to which it sells fast-memory chips.\par \par \uc2 Micron, along with Korea's \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  and \uc2 SK Hynix\uc2 , are to AI what oil producers are to the airlines: makers of an essential input that this year suddenly became much more expensive.\par \par \uc2 Because there is extremely limited capacity to make the high-bandwidth memory that AI needs -- and it takes years to build new production facilities -- soaring data-center demand has simply jacked up prices. Micron's soaring profits are, for its customers, soaring costs.\par \par \uc2 We are witnessing an enormous transfer of cash from the providers of AI -- and, perhaps one day, AI users -- to the memory-chip makers. Profit shifts of this scale are rare events, and investors should be paying attention to where the money's coming from, where it's being spent and how long it will keep flowing.\par \par \uc2 In the quarter ending May 28, Micron increased prices for DRAM chips more than 60% on the previous three months, while increasing shipments by a low single-digit percentage, it said this week. Prices for NAND flash memory, also used in data centers, jumped more than 80%.\par \par \uc2 Usually, memory doesn't matter that much. But for Micron alone, customers paid $18 billion more. And that was just in the quarter. Prices have quadrupled in a year, and it's hurting outside AI, too.\par \par \uc2 Apple this week raised its prices for MacBooks more than 15%. Closer to home for me, the memory I bought on \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  a year ago to build a super-quiet computer (I hate fan noise) has tripled in price, and now costs more than the CPU. For an industry in which prices usually drop every year, it's a huge turnaround.\par \par \uc2 In consumer electronics, passing on higher prices helps limit demand for chips -- just as higher oil prices reduce consumption. But the AI companies aren't passing on higher prices, because they are able to throw money at supply problems.\par \par \uc2 The problem in AI is that the end users aren't covering the costs of the service, with big losses recorded by the leading AI-model producers, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 . Everything is still priced to bring in new customers, not yet to make money. So higher input costs create a nasty problem: Either losses will be even bigger, or higher prices will be needed, putting off potential customers.\par \par \uc2 Already big companies have begun rationing AI as employee usage of the "tokens" that measure chatbot and agent use skyrockets, without necessarily helping productivity. Pushing up the price of the tokens in this environment will only slow adoption.\par \par \uc2 Given that investors are, for now, focused on market share rather than the bottom line, I wouldn't expect the higher costs to be passed on to AI users.\par \par \uc2 That means they will be absorbed either by the data-center providers or the model producers. Total profits for the AI "stack" of businesses critical to supplying large language models remain about the same, but margins for buyers of chips are hurt and more of the profit is made by the chip suppliers.\par \par \uc2 This might already be showing up in the stock market, although the speculation in microchip shares is so extreme it's hard to be sure.\par \par \uc2 This year, shares in Micron and \uc2 SK Hynix\uc2  are both up about 290% in dollar terms, and \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  is up 166%, while the so-called hyperscaler cloud providers of \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Meta\uc2  are down, \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's flat and \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 's only up 8%.\par \par \uc2 Where it really shows up is in the performance since April, when the memory stocks accelerated upward. All the hyperscalers are down since then, along with \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , which packages memory chips with its graphics processors for AI.\par \par \uc2 This could just be traders shifting money out of the last big wave of AI gains to chase soaring chip stocks. But it fits the idea of profits shifting from high up in the AI stack down to the memory-chip suppliers.\par \par \uc2 Even some bullish investors worry about the scale of speculation in chip stocks. Alec Cutler, a fund manager at \uc2 Orbis\uc2 , argues that the increased complexity of manufacturing and reduced number of competitors means there is no need for concern about new supply -- my main worry, and the usual cause of collapsing chip prices and profits.\par \par \uc2 However, he's sold his longstanding holding in \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  in favor of its preferred shares, which normally track closely but have lagged behind the ordinary stock during the boom this year, and particularly since April.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, there are only three ways to deal with higher prices from chip suppliers, as the airlines can attest when oil rises. Make lower profits (the short-term response), find efficiencies so you need less (in the long run), or wait for more supply (as fat profits encourage production).\par \par \uc2 All three are likely in AI, and investors need to think carefully about which parts of the AI stack will make money, and how long it will last.\par \par \uc2 Write to James Mackintosh at james.mackintosh@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (June 29, 2026).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 29, 2026 02:33 ET (06:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Document DJDN000020260629em6t000ht\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
