\par\par \b \uc2 South Korea Announces Massive New Chip, AI Investment Plan -- Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kwanwoo Jun \par \uc2 512 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2026\par 10:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2026, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 South Korea has laid out plans for nearly $2 trillion of investment in semiconductor industries and \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial-intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  infrastructure, with memory-chip leaders \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  and \uc2 SK Hynix\uc2  committing to substantial new investments.\par \par \uc2 More than 3 quadrillion won will be spent in the coming years to create fresh chip and AI industry hubs in South Korea.\par \par \uc2 The expansion aims to deepen South Korea's role in the AI supply chain at a time when semiconductor chips are experiencing a boom in demand due partly to the global rollout of data centers. It also aims to fend off competition in production of advanced chips, including from the U.S. and China.\par \par \uc2 SK Hynix\uc2  and \uc2 Samsung\uc2 --alongside \uc2 Micron Technology\uc2  of the U.S.--are the world's leading suppliers of high bandwidth memory chips, which are crucial parts of \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 's AI accelerators and other advanced memory products for AI applications.\par \par \uc2 The two Korean chip makers have become trillion-dollar companies as institutional and mom-and-pop investors have piled into their stock, with the latter group often relying on debt to fund purchases. South Korea is the best-performing share market in the world this year, but the dominance of the two companies has fueled wild swings in local equities that have unsettled regulators and led to occasional trading halts to steady nerves.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's industry minister said at a televised government briefing on Monday that he expects the government and companies to invest around 800 trillion won to build new chip-making hubs in southwest Korea, 81 trillion won to build chip-packaging facilities in central Korea and 30 trillion won to secure an early foothold in emerging chip markets over the next 15 years.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the nation's science minister said he expects more than 1 quadrillion won to be invested by both the government and companies in building AI data centers by 2035.\par \par \uc2 The briefing was chaired by President \uc2 Lee Jae Myung\uc2 , who has called for large-scale investment in South Korea's semiconductor and AI industries, including robotics and AI data centers, while pledging strong government support for relevant businesses.\par \par \uc2 SK Group\uc2  Chairman Chey Tae-won said mobile carrier \uc2 SK Telecom\uc2  plans to build 15 gigawatts of AI data center capacity in Korea.\par \par \uc2 Chey also said \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  supplier \uc2 SK Hynix\uc2  plans to invest 1.100 quadrillion won to expand its chip production in Korea in the future, and of the total, 400 trillion won has been earmarked for investment in a new facility in southwest Korea.\par \par \uc2 Samsung\uc2  Chairman Lee Jae-yong said that he has picked Gwangju in southwest Korea as a strong candidate to host a new \uc2 Samsung\uc2  chip production hub. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  said in a separate statement that it and other AI-related \uc2 Samsung\uc2  affiliates are also expanding their investments across the country, worth 2.655 quadrillion won in total.\par \par \uc2 Write to Kwanwoo Jun at kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 29, 2026 04:54 ET (08:54 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Document DJDN000020260629em6t000rg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
